  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Jimmy Buffett learned to hold an audience while performing in the streets and bars of New Orleans. Work as a journalist at Billboard sharpened the observational storytelling that also shaped his songs. He carried that instinct into books, becoming one of the few writers to reach the top of both fiction and nonfiction bestseller lists.\n\nBuffett’s business ambitions grew from wanting control over his own name. After seeing unauthorized merchandise, he opened a Margaritaville shop with his friend Donna Smith in 1985. The venture evolved into a licensing operation with John Cohlan, allowing Buffett to extend his creative identity beyond the stage while retaining an ownership stake.",
    "html": "<p>Jimmy Buffett learned to hold an audience while performing in the streets and bars of New Orleans. Work as a journalist at Billboard sharpened the observational storytelling that also shaped his songs. He carried that instinct into books, becoming one of the few writers to reach the top of both fiction and nonfiction bestseller lists.</p>\r\n<p>Buffett’s business ambitions grew from wanting control over his own name. After seeing unauthorized merchandise, he opened a Margaritaville shop with his friend Donna Smith in 1985. The venture evolved into a licensing operation with John Cohlan, allowing Buffett to extend his creative identity beyond the stage while retaining an ownership stake.</p>\r\n",
